Jahshaka patch 0061 — a voxeliser's region moves, and its OCTANTS move with it.

SOURCE patch. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after
pulling this. It touches `Components/Hlms/Pbs/{include,src}/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.{h,cpp}`,
which no other patch in the stack touches.

THE DEFECT, and it is the mechanism behind a lane's two red suites.
`VctVoxelizer::dividideOctants()` COPIES `mRegionToVoxelize` into every octant's
own `Aabb`, and `build()` then uses that COPY twice:

  * `placeItemsInBuckets()` culls the scene's items against `octant.region`
    (OgreVctVoxelizer.cpp:1082), and
  * the dispatch passes `octant.region.getMinimum()` as `voxelOrigin` (:1467) —
    the world point the voxelisation shader writes from.

`setRegionToVoxelize()` replaced `mRegionToVoxelize` and left the octants
describing the box that had just been replaced. So a caller that MOVES a
voxeliser's region — which is the whole shape of a camera-following cascade —
and rebuilds gets: the geometry of the OLD box, voxelised at the OLD origin,
into a texture that `VctLighting` maps onto the NEW one (`fillConstBufferData`
reads `getVoxelOrigin()` LIVE, so the shader's mapping is correct while the
contents are not). A wrong bounce, permanently, with no log line, no validation
error and no difference in cost — and a from-scratch rebuild, which makes a new
voxeliser, hides it by curing it.

IT IS THE CLASS'S OWN INVARIANT, BROKEN IN ONE OF TWO SIBLINGS.
`VctImageVoxelizer::setRegionToVoxelize` ends with `mOctants.clear()`
(OgreVctImageVoxelizer.cpp:629) for exactly this reason, and its caller
re-divides before building (`buildRelative`, :1095). The rasterising voxeliser
never did either, and nothing in its header says a caller must — so every caller
that moves a region has been silently wrong since the class was written.
Upstream's own `VctCascadedVoxelizer` does not meet it because it drives the
IMAGE voxeliser.

MEASURED (lane PHOTON-E2, spikes/photon-e2/FINDINGS-history.md). With Photon's
cascade chain the same document rendered 42,42,42 in the session that created it
and 47,47,47 after a save/close/reopen — because the first session's cascades had
SCROLLED (the camera moved between the build and the picture) and the second's
were built where they stood. Every term was ruled out by measurement first: the
document (node-for-node and field-for-field identical), the probe grid (a full
re-capture changes nothing), the bounce count (it reproduces at one bounce), the
irradiance field (it reproduces with the field off), the cascade boxes, item
counts and attach sets (identical), and `world.refreshGi()` (it does not correct
it). What remained was the voxel content, and the split is 0 with this patch.

THE FIX RE-DERIVES RATHER THAN CLEARS, which is the difference between this and
its sibling: `dividideOctants` remembers its division, and `setRegionToVoxelize`
re-runs it. A bare `mOctants.clear()` here would leave `build()` with an empty
octant list — an assert in a debug build and a silently BLACK volume in a release
one — for every existing caller that does not know to re-divide. Re-deriving
keeps them all working and costs a handful of float operations on a call that
already precedes a voxelisation.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0060 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane
worktree. `--engine-selftest` MOVES with it, and that is the defect leaving: the
default scene's own chain scrolls once as the first frames are drawn
(0cce4821… before, the lane's report carries the new hash and its A/B). The
suites are `scene.reopen_fidelity` (a document renders the same before and after
a save) and `gi.cascade_determinism` (a cascade built at a place and a cascade
SCROLLED to it are the same picture) — the second is new and reds on the
pre-patch pin.

--- a/Components/Hlms/Pbs/include/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.h
+++ b/Components/Hlms/Pbs/include/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.h
@@ -243,6 +243,12 @@
         };
 
         FastArray<Octant> mOctants;
+        /// The division `dividideOctants` was last called with, so that moving the
+        /// region can re-derive the octants from the SAME division instead of
+        /// leaving them describing the box that has just been replaced.
+        /// 0 = never divided, which is the only state in which a move has nothing
+        /// to re-derive.
+        uint32 mNumOctantsX, mNumOctantsY, mNumOctantsZ;
 
         ResourceTransitionArray mResourceTransitions;
 
--- a/Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.cpp
+++ b/Components/Hlms/Pbs/src/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.cpp
@@ -117,7 +117,10 @@
                                        Root::getSingleton().getCompositorManager2(),
                                        renderSystem->getTextureGpuManager() ) ),
         mAutoRegion( true ),
-        mMaxRegion( Aabb::BOX_INFINITE )
+        mMaxRegion( Aabb::BOX_INFINITE ),
+        mNumOctantsX( 0u ),
+        mNumOctantsY( 0u ),
+        mNumOctantsZ( 0u )
     {
         memset( mComputeJobs, 0, sizeof( mComputeJobs ) );
         memset( mAabbCalculator, 0, sizeof( mAabbCalculator ) );
@@ -890,6 +893,25 @@
         mAutoRegion = autoRegion;
         mRegionToVoxelize = regionToVoxelize;
         mMaxRegion = maxRegion;
+
+        // THE OCTANTS DESCRIBE THE REGION, so a region that moves takes them with it.
+        // dividideOctants() COPIES mRegionToVoxelize into every octant's own Aabb, and
+        // build() then uses that copy twice: placeItemsInBuckets() culls the items
+        // against it, and the dispatch passes its minimum as `voxelOrigin` — the world
+        // point the voxelisation shader writes from. Leave them behind and the next
+        // build() voxelises the geometry of the OLD box, at the OLD origin, into a
+        // texture that VctLighting maps onto the NEW one (fillConstBufferData reads
+        // getVoxelOrigin() live): a wrong bounce, permanently, with no log line, no
+        // validation error and no cost difference.
+        //
+        // VctImageVoxelizer::setRegionToVoxelize already ends with mOctants.clear() for
+        // exactly this reason, and its caller re-divides before building — so this class
+        // was the one of the two that did not keep the invariant. Re-deriving (rather
+        // than clearing) keeps every existing caller working: a clear would leave
+        // build() with an empty octant list, which is an assert in debug and a silently
+        // BLACK volume in release.
+        if( mNumOctantsX )
+            dividideOctants( mNumOctantsX, mNumOctantsY, mNumOctantsZ );
     }
     //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
     void VctVoxelizer::autoCalculateRegion()
@@ -1248,6 +1270,12 @@
     //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
     void VctVoxelizer::dividideOctants( uint32 numOctantsX, uint32 numOctantsY, uint32 numOctantsZ )
     {
+        // Remembered so that setRegionToVoxelize can re-derive them when the region
+        // moves (see the note there).
+        mNumOctantsX = numOctantsX;
+        mNumOctantsY = numOctantsY;
+        mNumOctantsZ = numOctantsZ;
+
         mOctants.clear();
         mOctants.reserve( numOctantsX * numOctantsY * numOctantsZ );
 
